/* Manually authored QA fixture from the supplied paper, not a live model reply. */
const price=[10,12,14,16,18,20,22,24,26],demand=[99,95,90,85,80,75,70,65,60];
const english=[78,65,82,70,91,68,75,88,60,84],spanish=[80,62,85,74,89,70,73,92,65,81];
const rank=v=>v.map(x=>[...v].sort((a,b)=>b-a).indexOf(x)+1);
const rx=rank(english),ry=rank(spanish);
const blocks=[];
const h=text=>blocks.push({type:'heading',text,level:1});
const p=text=>blocks.push({type:'paragraph',text});
const f=text=>blocks.push({type:'formula',text});
const table=(headers,rows)=>blocks.push({type:'table',headers,rows});
h('Question 1 — Price and demand');
p('Let x denote price in rupees and y denote demand in units. There are nine paired observations. We use Pearson correlation to measure the strength and direction of their linear association; the two regression equations predict different dependent variables.');
table(['x','y','x²','y²','xy'],price.map((x,i)=>[x,demand[i],x*x,demand[i]**2,x*demand[i]]).concat([['162','719','3156','58901','12350']]));
p('The last row contains column totals. Thus n = 9, Σx = 162, Σy = 719, Σx² = 3156, Σy² = 58901 and Σxy = 12350. All calculations below use these same observations.');
h('1(i) — Pearson correlation');
p('Step 1. Compute the three corrected sums in the numerator and denominator. They account for the sample means; using only Σxy would not measure correlation correctly.');
f('A = nΣx² − (Σx)² = 9 × 3156 − 162² = 2160');
f('B = nΣy² − (Σy)² = 9 × 58901 − 719² = 13148');
f('C = nΣxy − ΣxΣy = 9 × 12350 − 162 × 719 = −5328');
p('Step 2. Substitute these corrected sums into Pearson’s formula and evaluate the square root before dividing.');
f('r = C / √(AB) = −5328 / √(2160 × 13148) = −0.999787');
p('Step 3. The value is negative and extremely close to −1, indicating an almost perfect negative linear association: higher prices accompany lower demand in this sample. Correlation alone does not prove causation.');
h('1(ii) — Demand on price, with price Rs. 30');
p('Step 1. Calculate the sample means and the slope for predicting demand from price. The denominator must use the variation in price, the predictor.');
f('x̄ = Σx/n = 162/9 = 18; ȳ = Σy/n = 719/9 = 79.888889');
f('b(y on x) = C/A = −5328/2160 = −2.466666667');
p('Step 2. The fitted line passes through the pair of sample means. Use this property to obtain its intercept.');
f('a = ȳ − bx̄ = 79.888889 − (−2.466666667 × 18) = 124.288889');
f('Predicted demand: ŷ = 124.288888889 − 2.466666667x');
p('Step 3. Substitute x = 30 into the unrounded line, then round the prediction.');
f('ŷ(30) = 124.288888889 − 2.466666667 × 30 = 50.288889');
p('Estimated demand is approximately 50.29 units. Price 30 is beyond the observed range 10–26, so this is an extrapolation and should be interpreted cautiously.');
h('1(iii) — Price on demand, with demand 72');
p('Step 1. To predict price from demand, exchange the roles of predictor and response. This requires a separately fitted line; simply solving the previous regression line for x is generally incorrect.');
f('b(x on y) = C/B = −5328/13148 = −0.405232735');
f('a = x̄ − bȳ = 18 − (−0.405232735 × 79.888889) = 50.373593');
p('Step 2. Substitute demand y = 72 into the price-on-demand line.');
f('x̂ = 50.373592942 − 0.405232735y');
f('x̂(72) = 50.373592942 − 0.405232735 × 72 = 21.196836');
p('The estimated price is Rs. 21.20. Retaining full precision through substitution avoids small errors caused by rounding the displayed slope too early.');
h('1(iv) — Comment');
p('Both fitted slopes are negative, consistent with the negative correlation. As a consistency check, the product of the two regression slopes equals r². The association is almost linear for these observations, but the fitted lines remain sample-based predictions rather than a universal demand law.');
h('Question 2(a) — Spearman rank correlation');
p('Step 1. Rank the ten English scores and the ten Spanish scores separately, assigning rank 1 to the highest score in both subjects. There are no tied scores within either subject, so the ordinary no-ties Spearman formula applies.');
table(['Student','English','Spanish','Rank E','Rank S','d','d²'],english.map((e,i)=>[i+1,e,spanish[i],rx[i],ry[i],rx[i]-ry[i],(rx[i]-ry[i])**2]));
p('Step 2. Subtract the ranks row by row and square each difference. The differences are 0, −1, 1, 1, −1, 0, −1, 1, 1, −1, and their squared sum is 8.');
f('Σd² = 0 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 8');
p('Step 3. Substitute n = 10 and Σd² = 8 into Spearman’s formula.');
f('ρ = 1 − 6Σd²/[n(n² − 1)]');
f('ρ = 1 − (6 × 8)/[10 × (100 − 1)] = 1 − 48/990 = 0.951515');
p('The rank correlation is approximately 0.9515, indicating a strong positive association between the students’ relative performance in English and Spanish.');
h('Question 2(b)(i) — Partial correlation');
p('Given r₁₂ = 0.75, r₁₃ = 0.60 and r₂₃ = 0.50, we seek the association between sales and advertising after controlling linearly for price.');
f('r₁₂.₃ = (r₁₂ − r₁₃r₂₃) / √[(1 − r₁₃²)(1 − r₂₃²)]');
p('Step 1. Compute the numerator and denominator separately.');
f('Numerator = 0.75 − 0.60 × 0.50 = 0.45');
f('Denominator = √[(1 − 0.36)(1 − 0.25)] = √0.48 = 0.692820323');
p('Step 2. Divide the two quantities and interpret the sign.');
f('r₁₂.₃ = 0.45/0.692820323 = 0.649519');
p('Controlling for price leaves a positive partial correlation of approximately 0.6495 between sales and advertising. This is an association after adjustment, not proof of a causal advertising effect.');
h('Question 2(b)(ii) — Multiple correlation');
p('Use the multiple correlation formula for sales as the response and advertising plus price as predictors. The multiple correlation coefficient is the nonnegative square root.');
f('R₁.₂₃² = (r₁₂² + r₁₃² − 2r₁₂r₁₃r₂₃)/(1 − r₂₃²)');
f('Numerator = 0.5625 + 0.36 − 2 × 0.75 × 0.60 × 0.50 = 0.4725');
f('R₁.₂₃² = 0.4725/0.75 = 0.63; R₁.₂₃ = √0.63 = 0.793725');
p('The multiple correlation is approximately 0.7937. Its square, 0.63, is the proportion of sample variance represented by the corresponding linear regression under this correlation setup.');
h('Question 3 — Flight reservations');
p('Step 1. Let K be the number of the five uncertain passengers who arrive. The seven regular commuters arrive with certainty. Independence and equal arrival probability 0.5 give K ~ Binomial(5, 0.5), and total arrivals are 7 + K.');
f('P(K = k) = C(5,k)(0.5)^k(0.5)^(5 − k) = C(5,k)/32');
p('Part (i), step 2. Overbooking means more than ten passengers arrive. Therefore 7 + K > 10, which is equivalent to K ≥ 4. Exactly ten arrivals do not overbook the flight.');
f('P(overbooked) = P(K = 4) + P(K = 5) = (5 + 1)/32 = 0.1875');
p('Part (ii), step 2. At least one empty seat means at most nine arrivals. Therefore 7 + K ≤ 9, equivalent to K ≤ 2.');
f('P(empty seat) = [C(5,0) + C(5,1) + C(5,2)]/32');
f('P(empty seat) = (1 + 5 + 10)/32 = 0.5');
p('The probabilities are 18.75% for overbooking and 50% for at least one empty seat. The strict inequalities matter: “not overbooked” also includes exactly ten arrivals and is not the same as an empty seat.');
h('Question 4(i) — Absolute difference of normal variables');
p('Step 1. The given 2 and 1.5 are standard deviations, not variances. For independent normal variables, D = X − Y is normal with mean 35 − 42 = −7 and variance 2² + 1.5² = 6.25. Thus its standard deviation is 2.5.');
f('D ~ N(mean −7, variance 6.25); Z = (D + 7)/2.5');
p('Step 2. The event |D| ≥ 1 consists of two disjoint tails: D ≤ −1 or D ≥ 1. Standardise both bounds around the actual mean −7.');
f('z(−1) = (−1 + 7)/2.5 = 2.4; z(1) = (1 + 7)/2.5 = 3.2');
p('Step 3. Add the lower-tail probability at 2.4 and the upper-tail probability at 3.2. Equivalently subtract the probability between the two bounds from 1.');
f('P(|D| ≥ 1) = Φ(2.4) + [1 − Φ(3.2)]');
f('P(|D| ≥ 1) = 1 − [Φ(3.2) − Φ(2.4)] ≈ 0.992489602');
p('The answer is approximately 0.99249. The high probability is reasonable because D is centred at −7, well outside the interval from −1 to 1.');
h('Question 4(ii) — Matching two probabilities');
p('Step 1. Put S = X + 2Y. Its mean is 35 + 2 × 42 = 119 and, by independence, its variance is 2² + 4 × 1.5² = 13. Its standard deviation is √13.');
f('S ~ N(mean 119, variance 13)');
p('Step 2. Rewrite the requested equality using complements: P(|D| > 2λ) = P(|S| < λ). Since both distributions are continuous, equality at a boundary has probability zero. This equivalent equation avoids subtracting a tiny tail from 1.');
f('Left = Φ((7 − 2λ)/2.5) + [1 − Φ((7 + 2λ)/2.5)]');
f('Right = Φ((λ − 119)/√13) − Φ((−λ − 119)/√13)');
p('Step 3. Solve Left − Right = 0 numerically for λ > 0, using stable normal tail probabilities. Left decreases from 1 to 0 as λ grows, while Right increases from 0 to 1, so the crossing is unique. Bisection on the interval 0 to 100 gives the value below.');
f('λ ≈ 33.2340079034');
p('Step 4. Substitute this value into both complementary probabilities. Each is approximately 2.26528 × 10^−125; their ratio is approximately 1. The original probabilities both round to 1 at ordinary precision, which by itself is not evidence of equality.');
p('This numerical solution uses the statement exactly as printed. A large positive λ is not grounds for silently changing the question or assuming a different mean.');
h('Question 5 — Waiting times in a Poisson process');
p('Step 1. The arrival rate is 2 customers per minute. Over 3 minutes the mean count is 2 × 3 = 6, so N(3) ~ Poisson(6). Waiting beyond 3 minutes for an arrival can be expressed as a condition on this count.');
f('P(N(3) = k) = e^(−6) × 6^k/k!');
p('Part (i), step 2. The first customer arrives after 3 minutes exactly when no customers arrive in those 3 minutes.');
f('P(T₁ > 3) = P(N(3) = 0) = e^(−6) = 0.002478752');
p('Part (ii), step 2. The third customer arrives after 3 minutes exactly when the count by then is at most two. Sum the probabilities for k = 0, 1 and 2.');
f('P(T₃ > 3) = e^(−6)[1 + 6 + 6²/2!]');
f('P(T₃ > 3) = 25e^(−6) = 0.061968804');
p('The probabilities are approximately 0.00248 and 0.06197 respectively. The second event concerns the time of the third arrival, not a third independent exponential interval.');
h('Question 6 — Test of a defective-pair claim');
p('Step 1. Let p be the population defective proportion. Test H₀: p ≤ 0.05 against H₁: p > 0.05, using the null boundary p₀ = 0.05. The alternative is one-sided because the company claims “not more than” 5%.');
f('n = 400; defective count = 30; p̂ = 30/400 = 0.075');
p('Step 2. The null expected counts are np₀ = 20 and n(1 − p₀) = 380, allowing the usual large-sample normal approximation. Use the null proportion in the standard error.');
f('SE₀ = √[p₀(1 − p₀)/n] = √(0.05 × 0.95/400) = 0.010897247');
p('Step 3. Substitute the observed proportion into the one-proportion z statistic.');
f('z = (p̂ − p₀)/SE₀ = (0.075 − 0.05)/0.010897247 = 2.294157');
p('Step 4. At one-sided significance 0.05, reject when z > 1.64485. Here 2.29416 exceeds the critical value; the upper-tail p-value is approximately 0.01089, also below 0.05.');
f('P(Z ≥ 2.294157) ≈ 0.010890731 < 0.05');
p('Reject H₀ under this normal-approximation test. The sample provides evidence that the defective proportion exceeds 5%, so it does not support the company’s claim. This is a statistical conclusion at the stated significance level, not certainty about the exact population proportion.');
module.exports={price,demand,english,spanish,rx,ry,spec:{kind:'docx',title:'Statistics — complete worked solutions',style:{theme:'slate',font:'sans',border:'rule',pageSize:'A4'},blocks}};
